          Pregnancy is the one time when what is not on a protein powder label matters more than what is. You do need more protein, especially in the second half of pregnancy, but most of it can and should come from food. A powder earns a place only when nausea, food aversions, a vegetarian diet or a hectic schedule leave a real gap, and then the question is not which tub has the most protein but which one has been independently screened for contaminants and carries no herbs, stimulants or extra vitamins. Below are five picks that clear that bar, the protein numbers behind them, and the conversation to have with your OB, midwife or dietitian before you open any of them. This guide is general information, not medical advice.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-much" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Much Protein You Need During Pregnancy
          </h2>

          <p>
            The official numbers are a good place to start, because they are more modest than supplement marketing suggests. In the US Dietary Reference Intakes, the protein RDA for most adult women who are not pregnant is 46 g a day, or 0.8 g per kilogram of body weight. For pregnancy the Institute of Medicine raised it to 71 g a day, or about 1.1 g per kilogram, and applied the increase mainly to the second half of pregnancy, when most of the growth of the baby, placenta, blood volume and breast tissue happens. In the first trimester, requirements are treated as close to pre-pregnancy levels. The same 71 g figure, or about 1.3 g per kilogram, applies while breastfeeding.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a reasonable argument that the RDA undershoots. A small 2015 study in the Journal of Nutrition by Stephens and colleagues used a newer technique, indicator amino acid oxidation, in 29 healthy pregnant women and estimated requirements of about 1.2 g per kilogram in early pregnancy and about 1.5 g per kilogram late in pregnancy. That is meaningfully higher than the factorial estimate behind the RDA. The authors themselves called for larger studies to confirm it, so treat it as a signal that the upper end of a normal, protein-rich diet is sensible, not as a reason to chase high intakes with supplements.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, a woman who weighed 65 kg (about 143 lb) before pregnancy would land somewhere around 70 to 100 g a day in the second and third trimesters, depending on which estimate you use. That sounds like a lot until you add it up: a cup of Greek yogurt, two eggs, a portion of chicken or tofu, a glass of milk and a serving of beans covers most of it. An analysis of US national survey data published in 2021 found that roughly one in eight women in the second and third trimesters had protein intakes below the estimated requirement, so shortfalls happen, but they are the minority.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want a personal number, our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">guide to how much protein you need</a> explains the reasoning, and the <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> gives a starting point. During pregnancy, though, the right target is the one your OB, midwife or a registered dietitian sets with you, because twins, a low or high starting weight, kidney conditions, gestational diabetes and other factors change the answer.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">46 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Daily protein RDA, most non-pregnant women</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">71 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Daily RDA in later pregnancy and breastfeeding</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1.1 g/kg</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pregnancy RDA per kg of body weight</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~1.5 g/kg</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Late-pregnancy estimate from one small 2015 study</p>
              </div>
