          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked: Screening First, Protein Second
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run lab tests, taste panels or trials for this guide, and we did not test these products on pregnant women. The picks come from label analysis, publicly listed third-party certifications and published research, the same method described on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test page</a>. Where we mention taste or texture, we are passing on what buyers commonly report.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most of our buying guides, protein per calorie or cost per 25 g decides the ranking. Here the order of priorities is different. The first filter was independent screening: a current NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice listing that the brand or certifier publishes, which means an outside lab checks products for banned substances and, in NSF's case, for label accuracy and contaminants such as heavy metals. The second was a short, fully disclosed ingredient list with no proprietary blends. The third was the absence of anything a pregnant woman would need to clear separately with her provider: no herbs or botanicals, no caffeine or other stimulants, no fat burners, and no added vitamin A or large vitamin and mineral premixes that could double up with a prenatal vitamin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Only after those filters did we look at protein per serving, digestibility and price. Dairy proteins dominate the list because whey and milk proteins are complete, easy to digest for most people and, as a group, have tested lower for lead than plant proteins in independent testing. We included one pea-based product made for pregnancy because some women avoid dairy, and it carries a sport certification and brand heavy-metal testing. We left out powders whose main selling point is an extra ingredient, including collagen-only products, which are an incomplete protein, and lactation blends built around herbs.
          </p>

          <p>
            A certification seal reduces risk; it does not make a product risk-free or approve it for pregnancy. None of these certifiers evaluates whether a product is appropriate for pregnant women. That judgment belongs to you and your clinician, which is why every pick below comes with the same instruction: check the label, look up the lot, and ask first.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Certified, short label, nothing extra.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If a powder is not independently screened, or if it contains herbs, stimulants or added vitamin A, it does not belong in a pregnancy routine, however good the protein numbers look. Food comes first, and any powder gets your provider's sign-off.</p>
