        <div id="heavy-metals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Heavy Metals and Why Third-Party Testing Matters Most Now
          </h2>

          <p>
            Lead is the contaminant that matters most here. Public health agencies, including the CDC, say there is no known safe level of lead exposure, and lead can cross the placenta, which is why pregnancy is the time to minimize avoidable sources. Protein powders are one such source. Dietary supplements in the US are not approved by the FDA before they go on sale, and the protein ingredients themselves, especially plant proteins grown in soil, can carry traces of lead, cadmium and arsenic.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most widely reported recent test came from Consumer Reports in October 2025. It tested 23 protein powders and ready-to-drink shakes and found that more than two-thirds contained more lead in a single serving than its experts' daily level of concern. Plant-based products averaged about nine times as much lead as dairy-based ones, and two vegan products were so far over the threshold that Consumer Reports advised avoiding them altogether. Dairy-based products had the lowest levels overall, although even some of those were above the publication's limit for daily use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Context matters. Consumer Reports' level of concern, 0.5 micrograms of lead a day, is deliberately conservative, and the supplement industry's trade group pointed out that exceeding it is not the same as exceeding a government safety limit. Lead also comes from many sources, including old paint, water pipes and everyday foods. The practical takeaway is not panic but selection: if you use a powder during pregnancy, use one that has been independently screened, lean toward dairy-based protein unless you avoid dairy, and keep it to a single serving a day rather than a habit of several shakes.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also pays to know what each seal covers. NSF Certified for Sport tests for banned substances, verifies that the label matches the contents and, according to NSF, checks for harmful levels of contaminants such as heavy metals. Informed Sport and Informed Choice focus mainly on banned-substance screening of batches. Some brands publish their own heavy-metal results or certificates of analysis on request. Before you buy, search the product, and ideally the lot number on the tub, in the certifier's online database. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> walks through what testing does and does not prove, and our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> guide ranks products on contaminant screening first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="avoid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Ingredients to Avoid: Herbs, Caffeine and High-Dose Vitamin A
          </h2>

          <p>
            The protein in a powder is rarely the problem during pregnancy. The extras are. Start with herbs and botanicals. Many powders, including some marketed to women, add adaptogen blends, greens powders, turmeric, ashwagandha, maca or similar ingredients. Most have little or no safety research in pregnancy, and some, such as ashwagandha, are flagged by federal health resources as possibly unsafe in pregnancy. Lactation-support powders often contain fenugreek, which is not recommended during pregnancy beyond the amounts found in food. Even an otherwise excellent certified product can include a small recovery blend; that is a reason to read the label and ask, not necessarily to panic.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next is caffeine. The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists advises keeping caffeine below about 200 mg a day during pregnancy. Coffee-flavored, mocha and energy-style protein products sometimes contain real coffee or added caffeine, and pre-workout or fat-burning formulas can contain much more, along with stimulants such as synephrine or yohimbe that have no place in pregnancy. Choose plain, vanilla or chocolate flavors, check for caffeine on the label, and count any you do drink toward your daily total.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third is vitamin A. Preformed vitamin A, listed as retinol or retinyl palmitate or acetate, can cause birth defects at high intakes, and the Tolerable Upper Intake Level for adults, including during pregnancy, is 3,000 micrograms RAE (10,000 IU) a day. Your prenatal vitamin already contains a measured amount. Meal-replacement shakes and fortified protein drinks often add vitamin A and other vitamins, so a prenatal, a fortified shake and a diet that includes liver could add up faster than you expect. Beta-carotene from plants does not carry the same risk. The simplest approach is to choose a protein powder with no added vitamins at all and let your prenatal do that job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, be wary of anything sold for weight loss, detox, cleansing or appetite control, which pregnancy is not the time for, and of proprietary blends that list ingredients without amounts. Sweeteners are a smaller issue: approved sweeteners such as sucralose and stevia extracts are considered safe at typical intakes by regulators, but sugar alcohols can worsen bloating, and some women simply prefer unflavored. If your stomach is sensitive, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-sensitive-stomach/">best protein powder for sensitive stomach</a> guide covers gentler options.
          </p>

